# =============================================================================
# Lumina Literature — environment variables
# =============================================================================
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
# -------------
# 1. Copy this file to ".env"  (just the name, no extension)
#       cp .env.example .env
# 2. Fill in the real values in .env.
# 3. .env is in .gitignore and will NEVER be committed to GitHub.
#    Only .env.example (this file) is committed — as a template.
# 4. For production, add the same variables in Vercel:
#       Vercel Dashboard → Project → Settings → Environment Variables
#    Vercel injects them at build time; do NOT commit production values here.
#
# THE VITE PREFIX RULE — IMPORTANT
# --------------------------------
# Vite only exposes env vars prefixed with VITE_ to the browser bundle.
# - VITE_FOO  → accessible in client code as import.meta.env.VITE_FOO
#               AND visible to anyone who views the JS source
# - FOO       → only available at build time on the server; NEVER reaches the
#               browser. (Lumina has no server today, so this rule is moot now
#               but matters once a serverless function or build script lands.)
#
# RULE: never put a service-role key, signing secret, admin token, or anything
# you wouldn't tweet, into a VITE_ variable. Use the unprefixed form (and a
# server) for those.
#
# =============================================================================

# --- Supabase (planned) ------------------------------------------------------
# When Supabase lands, the "anon" public key is safe in the client bundle
# because Row Level Security (RLS) policies enforce access on the database
# side. The service-role key is NEVER safe in the client.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...  # public, RLS-gated
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=...         # server-only, never VITE_

# --- Firebase Auth (decision pending) ----------------------------------------
# The Firebase web config is all public-ish — Firebase enforces auth via
# rules. Still keep it in env so you can rotate without code changes.
# VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
# VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# --- Anything else -----------------------------------------------------------
# Add new variables above this line. Keep VITE_-prefixed vars at the top of
# each section so it's obvious which ones reach the browser.
